# nr-office-pdf-analysis: what the lead applies (all of it is in files this task does not own)
# Written without cargo: nothing of the new crate mixdog-office-pdf has been compiled or run in Rust (the recording itself
# was produced by running Node over generated PDFs).

## 1. native/core/Cargo.toml
[workspace] members: add, right after `"crates/mixdog-office-base",`:

    "crates/mixdog-office-pdf",

[workspace.dependencies]: add, right after the `mixdog-office-base = { path = "crates/mixdog-office-base" }` line:

    mixdog-office-pdf = { path = "crates/mixdog-office-pdf" }

## 2. native/core/Cargo.lock
Lead regenerates it offline (the build is --locked after that). The new package `mixdog-office-pdf` depends on `base64`,
`mixdog-fsio`, `mixdog-js`, `mixdog-pdf`, `mixdog-raster`, `mixdog-workspace-hack` and `serde_json`; its dev-dependencies
are `mixdog-office-base` (feature `test-support`), `mixdog-testkit` and `serde_json`. All are already in the lock: no new
crate enters it, and no new workspace dependency edge other than the new crate's own.

## 3. native/core/crates/mixdog-workspace-hack/Cargo.toml
Nothing by hand: the new crate's Cargo.toml carries the `mixdog-workspace-hack` dependency as every office crate does.
Regenerate the hack if the workspace's hakari rule wants more features unified.

## 4. native/core/fixtures/MANIFEST.json
Index the new set `office-analysis` (recorder benchmarks/rust-core/recorders/record-office-analysis.mjs, 1 file,
golden.json; the set's own MANIFEST.json is written at native/core/fixtures/office-analysis/MANIFEST.json) with the
manifest tool that built the other entries: `node benchmarks/rust-core/recorders/verify-manifests.mjs --write-index`.

## 5. The crate's modules in lib.rs
The later tasks of the track (adapter, forms, render pages, writer, ocr, preview, content ops) each own their own files
of this crate and add their `pub mod` line to native/core/crates/mixdog-office-pdf/src/lib.rs, which this task wrote with
only its own modules (analysis, document, limits, search). `analysis::find_pdf_text` is re-exported for the adapter's
single import; the OCR exports of pdf-analysis.mjs (`ocrPdf`, `ocrTextLines`, `parseOcrBlocks`, `parseOcrTsv`,
`pdfOcrReadiness`) belong to the ocr task and are re-exported from its module.

## 6. Consumers
`mixdog_office_pdf::{open_pdf, selected_pages, PdfSource, AnalysisError}` (document.rs: the opening with the office
options and the page selection with Node's exact error texts), `extract_pdf_text_layout`, `infer_pdf_tables`,
`extract_pdf_images`, `extract_pdf_outline`, `find_pdf_text`, `MAX_PDF_ANALYSIS_PAGES` (limits.rs, shared with the
capability contract). Every result type has `to_json()` in the shape Node returns. A path source needs a
`mixdog_fsio::ReadProof` (the caller's lane proof).

## 7. SERVICE_PENDING (compose_flip.rs)
Nothing: this task registers no protocol method.

## 8. Known limits of what this crate reads, from the mixdog-pdf passes it stands on (none is added here)
- The operator list has no annotation appearance streams (the note in oplist/mod.rs), so the ruled lines and boxes that
  pdf.js reads out of a widget's appearance are not in `lines` and `boxes`.
- An inline image painted inside a form XObject is decoded with the page's resources, not the form's: only an image
  naming a colour space by a resource name can differ.
- The operator list is read without a font provider (nothing implements the seam yet): the analysis does not use shown
  text from it, but mixdog-pdf reports that once at error level on a page that shows text.
